tsbTheMachine
=============

tsbTheMachine is the ahead-of-time compiler and runtime for the mobile half of RapidFX and RapidJ.

It is a fork of RoboVM / MobiVM, maintained for our own purposes.


Origin
------

Derived from:

  MobiVM/robovm                      commit fed6c1164, 2026-08-10 ("iOS 26.2 bindings")
  thorstenstueker/robovm, br 27.0.0  commit f3babc9a5, 2026-09-28

The second is the first plus our own work: the class library filled in against Java 25, the VM core
ported to aarch64 Linux and to Android's Bionic, the CocoaTouch bindings reduced to what a rendered
user interface needs, and our own distribution and release pipeline.

RoboVM was created by Trillian Mobile AB / RoboVM AB. MobiVM is its community continuation. Neither
is affiliated with this fork, and neither endorses it.


Why it is a fork and not a contribution
---------------------------------------

The two projects are going in opposite directions, and that is not a complaint about either.

MobiVM's product is *native* user interfaces: its investment is in binding Apple's frameworks so
that a Java program can put UIKit controls on the screen. Ours is *one rendered* user interface
across desktop, web and mobile, drawn through a single painting interface. We had native peers once
and gave them up, because keeping three platforms' widget sets in agreement is the problem we are
trying to remove rather than solve.

So our changes are of no use upstream — for now — and upstream's yearly regeneration of the full
CocoaTouch tree is investment in the surface we are shrinking. Measured on 2026-09-28: this tree is
130 commits ahead of MobiVM's master and 0 behind it.

There is no obligation here to stay in step, and none is wanted. If a new Xcode or iOS SDK breaks
the bindings, what upstream did about it is worth a look — as a reference, not as a duty.


Licence
-------

GPL version 2, as RoboVM and MobiVM are. Per-module LICENSE.txt and NOTICE.txt files carry the
terms and the copyright notices of each part, and they are unchanged; parts of the runtime carry
the GPL's Classpath exception (compiler/rt/LICENSE_GPL_CE.txt) and parts are Apache 2.0, because
the runtime descends from Android's libcore, which descends from OpenJDK.

**Corresponding source.** Every released binary is built from a commit in this repository, and the
release tag names that commit. That is how the GPL's corresponding-source obligation is met: take
the tag, take the commit, and you have the source for the archive published beside it.

Releases made before this repository existed — tags `tsbmobile-27.0.0`, `tsbmobile-27.0.1` and
`tsbmobile-27.0.2` — live in `thorstenstueker/robovm`. That repository is archived and stays
readable, because binaries carrying those versions are in circulation and their source has to remain
reachable. `tsbmobile-27.0.0` is withdrawn: its robovm-rt.jar holds 502 duplicated classes that
iCloud wrote into the build tree, and 27.0.1 is the same commit line built from a swept tree.
